 To add a Gaussian filter before applying the Sobel operator, you can first create a Gaussian kernel with a certain standard deviation (sigma) using the `fspecial` function in MATLAB. Then, convolve the gray-scale image with the Gaussian kernel to smooth it out. After smoothing, you can proceed with applying the Sobel operators to find the edges.

Here's the modified code:

```matlab
% Load the input image
input_image = imread('image.jpg');

% Convert RGB image to grayscale
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);

% Define sigma value for Gaussian filter
sigma = 3;

% Create Gaussian kernel using fspecial
gaussian_kernel = fspecial('gaussian', [5 5], sigma);

% Smooth the gray-scale image using Gaussian filter
smoothed_image = conv2(gray_image, gaussian_kernel, 'same');

% Define Sobel operators in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];

% Apply Sobel operator in x direction to smoothed image
edge_image_x = conv2(double(smoothed_image), sobel_x, 'same');

% Apply Sobel operator in y direction to smoothed image
edge_image_y = conv2(double(smoothed_image), sobel_y, 'same');

% Combine edges in both directions and display result
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
imshow(edge_image);
```

