Here's the C code to convert `argv` (command-line arguments) to a null-terminated string:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    char *input = NULL;
    size_t input_size = 0;

    // Concatenate all command-line arguments into a single string
    for (int I = 0; I < argc; i++) {
        input_size += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // Add space for null terminator
        input = realloc(input, input_size); // Allocate more memory if necessary
        strcat(input, argv[i]); // Append argument to input string
    }

    printf("Input string: %s\n", input);

    free(input); // Free dynamically allocated memory
    return 0;
}
```

This program uses the `realloc()` function to allocate memory dynamically as it concatenates each command-line argument to the input string. The `strcat()` function is used to append each argument to the end of the input string. Finally, the resulting string is printed using `printf()`, and the dynamically allocated memory is freed using `free()`. Note that this program assumes that the total length of all command-line arguments will fit within a single `char` array. If you anticipate very long command-lines, you may need to adjust the logic accordingly.

